# Pairs with UniqueOwner. The reshard drops its interlock with an in-flight
# resize, so the split it drives commits on T during the resize, with the same
# outcome as UniqueOwnerSplitDuringResize. It shows a reshard's split is safe
# only because of the reshard's own interlock, which production does have.
#
# The base has no split-abandon path (its interlock makes one unreachable), so
# in this mutant a flip can strand a split mid-flight. A second edit lets that
# split count as finished, standing for AbandonRetargetedSplitAsync, and lets a
# reshard whose one split has been dispatched count as finished too, standing for
# the further splits production would dispatch (the instance has one). So the
# mutant cannot deadlock and the invariant is the only thing TLC can report.
MODULE: UniqueOwnerReshardDuringResize
TARGET: UniqueOwner
CLASS: Invariant
SUMMARY: a reshard starts during a resize, so its split leaves a stale router serving the split target after the flip
PERTURBS: ReshardStart

--- FIND
ReshardStart ==
    /\ rs = "idle"
    /\ sp = "idle"
    /\ rz \in {"idle", "purged", "undone"}
--- REPLACE
ReshardStart ==
    /\ rs = "idle"
    /\ sp = "idle"
\* MUTATION: no interlock with an in-flight resize.
--- END

--- FIND
    /\ sg \in {"idle", "done"}
    /\ sp \in {"idle", "done"}
--- REPLACE
    /\ sg \in {"idle", "done"}
\* MUTATION: a split the flip stranded counts as finished (AbandonRetargetedSplitAsync).
    /\ sp \in {"idle", "done"} \/ alias # spCopy
--- END

--- FIND
    /\ rs # "migrating"
    /\ late # "inflight"
--- REPLACE
\* MUTATION: so does a reshard whose one split has been dispatched.
    /\ rs # "migrating" \/ sp # "idle"
    /\ late # "inflight"
--- END
